I [wrote recently](/posts/20260330-context-was-always-the-job/) about how context was always the job — that the value of engineering was never in the syntax, it was in holding enough context to be precise about the right things. The more I've sat with that idea, the more I keep noticing it in other fields too.

The right course of action always depends on what you're trying to do. Build a car, and you need to know whether it should be fast or fuel-efficient or cheap. Write a news article, and you need to know who it's for and what they already understand. Give an AI agent a task, and you need to give it enough context to know what "good" looks like in your situation. This is obvious. But we keep acting as if the right answer is always the right answer — as if solutions are universal and context is a detail you fill in later.

My career has moved through journalism, content strategy, information architecture, design systems, and software engineering. I now spend most of my time on AI integration. I'm not an expert in all of these fields, but I've spent enough time in each to notice something: linguistic anthropology, software engineering, content strategy, and AI context engineering have all landed on structurally analogous problems around shared understanding. Different problems in detail, but with a striking family resemblance. Each one spent years working through the hard parts. And they're barely leveraging each other's lessons.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - At least four traditions have landed on structurally analogous problems around shared understanding — different in detail, but with striking family resemblance
> - They share intellectual ancestry in places but developed their solutions largely in parallel, and are barely leveraging each other's lessons
> - AI is making the convergence visible because context failures with LLMs are instant and obvious
> - If you're treating context as a domain-specific concern, you're missing transferable solutions from adjacent fields

## The threads

**Linguistic anthropology (1992).** Alessandro Duranti and Charles Goodwin published [*Rethinking Context*](https://web.archive.org/web/20030312200748/http://www.sscnet.ucla.edu/anthro/faculty/duranti/reprints/rethco.pdf), which changed how linguists think about context. Their argument: context isn't static background to communication. It's actively produced through interaction. People don't just operate within a context; they build it together as they talk, gesture, and respond.

<aside class="kh-marginnote">Paul Dourish <a href="https://www.dourish.com/publications/2004/PUC2004-context.pdf">translated this thesis for computing in 2004</a>, arguing context is an "interactional problem" not a "representational problem." But the work landed in human-computer interaction (HCI), not in DDD, content strategy, or AI. Even when a bridge gets built, it stays within its own field.</aside>

**Software engineering (2003).** Eric Evans published [*Domain-Driven Design*](https://www.domainlanguage.com/ddd/), arguing that the hard problem of software is shared understanding of the domain, not the code. His prescription: build a "ubiquitous language" that domain experts and developers both use. Formalise it. Draw explicit boundaries ("bounded contexts") around different models so they don't corrupt each other. The primary artifact of engineering, in Evans' framing, is the shared model, not the running system. I didn't encounter Evans until years after I'd been doing content modelling, and the overlap is striking.

**Content strategy (2009--2012).** Daniel Jacobson at NPR built [COPE](https://www.programmableweb.com/news/cope-create-once-publish-everywhere/2009/10/13) (Create Once, Publish Everywhere): content modelled as structured data with standardised interfaces, not as presentation-specific blobs. Karen McGrane's [*Content Strategy for Mobile*](https://abookapart.com/products/content-strategy-for-mobile) sharpened the point: content that's trapped in a specific visual format ("blobs") can't travel across channels. You have to break it into meaningful "chunks" named by what they are, not how they look. "News item" survives a redesign; "featured card with sidebar layout" doesn't.

<aside class="kh-marginnote">McGrane's "blobs vs. chunks" and Evans' entities and value objects aren't the same problem — one is about content portability, the other about identity and lifecycle in domain models. But the structural move is the same: decompose opaque bundles into meaningful, named units. Content strategists could have borrowed Evans' formalisms; DDD practitioners could have learned from McGrane's portability patterns.</aside>

**AI context engineering (2025--2026).** The current wave. An LLM has general knowledge of the universe but not your project's constraints, your team's vocabulary, or why the module is shaped the way it is. The bottleneck isn't the model; it's [the context you provide](/posts/20251003-effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents/). Practitioners are building CLAUDE.md files, project briefs, scoped rules, and [structured documentation for AI consumption](/posts/20250929-ai-must-rtfm-context-curators/). The discipline is new. The problem it's solving is not.

I should be honest about the limits of this list. These aren't the only traditions working on how shared understanding crosses boundaries — knowledge management (Nonaka & Takeuchi's tacit-to-explicit conversion, 1995) and library/information science have deep roots here too. And the traditions I am covering aren't fully independent: content strategy grew out of computing and information architecture, AI context engineering is practiced almost entirely by software engineers, and DDD drew on earlier modelling traditions. Duranti and Goodwin are the genuinely independent thread. The others are parallel but adjacent, sharing intellectual ancestry while developing their solutions separately.

## What they share

The specific problems are different: DDD is about modelling business domains to reduce software complexity, content strategy is about portability across channels, linguistic anthropology is about how meaning emerges in interaction, and AI context engineering is about feeding the right information into a stateless model. But the structural patterns that emerged are strikingly similar:

**Context is produced, not inherited.** This is where Duranti and Goodwin started, and every tradition landed somewhere close. Evans' domain modelling is collaborative and iterative. McGrane's content schemas don't exist until someone defines them. AI context windows don't fill themselves. In every case, context is something you build on purpose or it doesn't exist.

**Context must be formalised to cross a boundary.** Evans' bounded contexts need explicit translation layers. McGrane's structured content needs schemas to travel between platforms. AI agents need structured context documents to work across sessions and tasks. Informal, tacit understanding works within a team. It breaks the moment you need to share across one. And the failure mode scales with speed: in software, [comprehension debt](/posts/20260328-digesting-comprehension-debt/) accumulates silently; in content, [syndication breaks](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/) compound slowly; in AI, hallucination is instant.

## The weaving has started, but it hasn't gone mainstream

<aside class="kh-marginnote">At <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2024/03/Evans-ddd-experiment-llm/">Explore DDD 2024</a>, Evans argued that a fine-tuned language model is essentially a bounded context. The DDD community is aware of AI, but mostly asking "how can AI help us do DDD?" rather than the reverse.</aside>

Different conferences, different journals, different vocabularies. Linguistic anthropologists don't attend DDD Europe. Content strategists don't read *Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture*. AI engineers building agent architectures aren't, for the most part, reading Karen McGrane.

Which is ironic, given that most of these fields argue for shared language as a core practice.

Birgitta Bockeler's [context engineering article](https://martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/context-engineering-coding-agents.html) on martinfowler.com is the most widely-read piece in this space right now. It describes practices that echo DDD without naming it: scoped rules tied to file types, project-specific conventions, skills as lazy-loaded context modules. It doesn't reference DDD once. Not because Bockeler doesn't know DDD, but because the AI context engineering conversation developed its own frame and didn't think to look sideways. The cost isn't a missing footnote; it's that twenty years of hard lessons about formalising shared understanding sit unused next door.

Meanwhile, Rod Johnson (creator of the Spring Framework) has been [arguing](https://medium.com/@springrod/context-engineering-needs-domain-understanding-b4387e8e4bf8) that context engineering is incomplete without domain modelling. His DICE framework argues that domain models should structure how context gets selected and organised, rather than treating the context window as an ever-growing string. A small but growing group is making this connection — Evans himself, [James Croft](https://www.jamescroft.co.uk/applying-domain-driven-design-principles-to-multi-agent-ai-systems/) at Microsoft, a handful of academic papers — but it hasn't penetrated the mainstream AI tooling ecosystem yet. And almost none of these voices are looking further afield to content strategy or linguistics.

## The AI moment as forcing function

Before AI, context failures were slow. A misaligned contractor takes weeks to deliver the wrong thing. A content schema that doesn't match across properties causes a slow bleed of broken syndication that nobody notices until it compounds. Comprehension debt accumulates silently while metrics stay green.

AI made the failure instant. Bad context in, bad output out, in seconds. The feedback loop that used to take weeks now takes a single prompt. That speed is what's making the bottleneck impossible to miss.

It's also why the discourse is converging now. When the cost of missing context was diffuse and slow, each tradition could afford to treat it as a domain-specific problem and solve it with domain-specific tools. When the failure is instant, the structural resemblance becomes hard to ignore, and the solutions start to look transferable.

## From where I sit

I'm not arguing these traditions should merge. I'm not even sure a unified theory would be useful. What I am doing is noting that the threads are visible from where I sit, and they haven't been woven together.

My recent posts on [context as infrastructure](/posts/20260330-context-was-always-the-job/), [content architecture](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/), and [comprehension debt](/posts/20260328-digesting-comprehension-debt/) each draw on one or two of these traditions. What I hadn't done until now is step back and notice the convergence itself.

If you're working on context engineering for AI and you haven't read Evans on ubiquitous language, there are twenty years of lessons on formalising shared understanding that you're rediscovering from scratch. If you're doing DDD and haven't looked at how content strategists model structured content across platforms, you're missing a parallel tradition that solved the "how does this travel across boundaries" problem for editorial organisations.

And if you're in any of these fields, Duranti and Goodwin is worth your time. That's where the deepest version of the argument lives.

The way I've come to see it: context is a verb, not a noun. It's something you actively build so that the pieces — people, systems, content, agents — can work together. None of these traditions own that insight. All of them arrived at it. The weaving is starting — but it hasn't reached mainstream practice in any of these fields yet. There's plenty left to pull together.

